# The backend this frontend is built and tested against. scripts/backend_jar.py reads this,
# builds what it names, and installs the result at neuralogic/jar/NeuraLogic.jar - which is no
# longer tracked. CI runs the same script, so this is one recipe a person and a workflow both
# execute.
#
#   repo   owner/name on GitHub, or a path to a local clone
#   ref    a commit, a branch or a tag - fetched and built as it stands
#
# Changing the backend and the frontend together: push the backend branch, point ref at it,
# push the frontend branch. CI builds that branch and tests this frontend against it. When the
# backend side merges, move ref to the commit it merged as.
#
#   ref  = my-feature-branch              a branch in the repo named below
#   repo = ../NeuraLogic                  a local clone, for a branch pushed nowhere yet
#   repo = LukasZahradnik/NeuraLogic      a fork, if that is where the work happens to be
#
# A commit is what should land here. --check compares one against the jar's own manifest stamp
# with no network at all; a branch it can only report on, because whether the installed jar is
# that branch's current tip is a question only the remote can answer.

repo = GustikS/NeuraLogic
ref = b0b65b55620b1ec1488231a3b904c916a593011d
